messages.content:
Analyze the Jira bug report title and description and the seed changes that were made to fix the reported bug. Combine this analysis with the initial static impact set, which contains the fully qualified names of code entities that are statically related to the seed changes.
For each entity in the initial static impact set, analyze its relationship to the seed changes and collect evidence indicating whether the entity must be modified as a consequence of the seed changes.  Furthermore, identify any additional code entities that must be modified or created and collect supporting evidence.
Focus on production code, not test code or other files.
Inputs: - Jira bug report title: FeatureGeneratorUtil.tokenFeature() always returns "lc" for Japanese words - Jira bug report description: FeatureGeneratorUtil.tokenFeature() always recognizes Japanese words as "lc" (lower case). It looks a bug to me because they're not lower case letters, but other than that, it seems that FeatureGeneratorUtil.tokenFeature() takes care only Europe/American languages.

For example, in Japanese NER problem, typical token classes are as follows:

- DIGIT
- HIRA : あ, い, う, え, お etc.
- KATA : ア, イ, ウ, エ, オ etc.
- ALPHA : we don't need to distinguish lower/upper case
- OTHER

I think it's possible that we get FeatureGeneratorUtil.tokenFeature() to have additional token classes I mentioned above, but later on, someone who comes from Asia and may claim similar thing.

I'd like to make FeatureGeneratorUtil plugable, but I don't have any idea now. - Seed changes and their Git diff hunks: [{"fqn": "opennlp.tools.util.featuregen.FeatureGeneratorUtil.tokenFeature(java.lang.String)", "git_diff_hunk": "@@ -59 +61,7 @@ public class FeatureGeneratorUtil {
- if (pattern.isAllLowerCaseLetter()) {
+ if (pattern.isAllHiragana()) {
+ feat = "jah";
+ }
+ else if (pattern.isAllKatakana()) {
+ feat = "jak";
+ }
+ else if (pattern.isAllLowerCaseLetter()) {"}] - Initial static code change impact set: ["opennlp.tools.namefind.DefaultNameContextGenerator.getContext(int,java.lang.String[],java.lang.String[],java.lang.Object[])", "opennlp.tools.util.featuregen.BigramNameFeatureGenerator.createFeatures(java.util.List,java.lang.String[],int,java.lang.String[])", "opennlp.tools.util.featuregen.BrownTokenClassFeatureGenerator.createFeatures(java.util.List,java.lang.String[],int,java.lang.String[])", "opennlp.tools.util.featuregen.FeatureGeneratorUtil.capPeriod", "opennlp.tools.util.featuregen.StringPattern.StringPattern(int,int)", "opennlp.tools.util.featuregen.StringPattern.containsComma()", "opennlp.tools.util.featuregen.StringPattern.containsDigit()", "opennlp.tools.util.featuregen.StringPattern.containsHyphen()", "opennlp.tools.util.featuregen.StringPattern.containsLetters()", "opennlp.tools.util.featuregen.StringPattern.containsPeriod()", "opennlp.tools.util.featuregen.StringPattern.containsSlash()", "opennlp.tools.util.featuregen.StringPattern.digits()", "opennlp.tools.util.featuregen.StringPattern.isAllCapitalLetter()", "opennlp.tools.util.featuregen.StringPattern.isAllHiragana()", "opennlp.tools.util.featuregen.StringPattern.isAllKatakana()", "opennlp.tools.util.featuregen.StringPattern.isAllLowerCaseLetter()", "opennlp.tools.util.featuregen.StringPattern.isInitialCapitalLetter()", "opennlp.tools.util.featuregen.StringPattern.recognize(java.lang.String)", "opennlp.tools.util.featuregen.TokenClassFeatureGenerator.createFeatures(java.util.List,java.lang.String[],int,java.lang.String[])", "opennlp.tools.util.featuregen.TokenPatternFeatureGenerator.createFeatures(java.util.List,java.lang.String[],int,java.lang.String[])", "opennlp.tools.util.featuregen.TrigramNameFeatureGenerator.createFeatures(java.util.List,java.lang.String[],int,java.lang.String[])"]

Expected output:
Structured evidence for each entity of the initial static impact set, including: - its fully qualified name - an assessment of whether it must be modified as a consequence of the seed changes - the supporting evidence
Additionally, include any code entities that must be modified or created but are not part of the initial static impact set.
